  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">EnRoute: Towards a living income of farmers to tackle child labour</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">After the baseline and development of Living Income strategies carried out in Project A (2020-2021), Project B will focus on Ivory Coast piloting different cash and service packages to uncover the relative merit of each.In two regions, Man and Daloa, households are divided into three groups and receive different packages for two years:1.	200 households receiving cash;2.	200 households receiving service interventions; and3.	200 households receiving cash and service interventions.The cash package consists of &#x20ac;200 per year for two years to the household, equally divided between the husband and wife and paid by mobile money.Households in group 1 receive a household vision training first, which helps the husband and wife to decide together what the cash is best spend on in their situation. The service packages consist of a smart mix of interventions, that was developed by Oxfam Novib, KIT and Beyond Beans in Project A.The package was designed to bridge the Living Income gap on all income drivers and addressing root causes of child labour (need for labour for plantation work and household chores).The service package consist of: &#x2022;	Foundational activities (VSLA-CHILD &#x2013; Village Savings &amp; Loans Associations with participatory training on entrepreneurship, gender and child protection),&#x2022;	Yield intensification (Farm Development Plan, Individual Coaching and Subsidised service groups),&#x2022;	Crop diversification (Income Generating Activity training and material distribution) and&#x2022;	Labour/cost saving (access to water points, improved cookstoves, and school fee credit).Aside farming households receiving three packages, there will be a control group to compare the impact.The project researches if there is a difference between the absolute impact in reducing the Living Income gap and reducing child labour between the packages. And the project also wants to understand the relative impact, e.g. which services work best, which groups benefit most from cash.</narrative>
  </description>
